Queue-saturation early termination for the HNSW base-layer search (bounded-queue path only), the baseline
of the paper's comparison with Patience (Teofili and Lin, "Patience in Proximity: A Simple Early Termination
Strategy for HNSW Graph Traversal in Approximate k-Nearest Neighbor Search", ECIR 2025). Apply after 0002.

SearchParametersHNSW gains patience_p, patience_s and patience_queue. The search keeps a cumulative count of
insertions into one queue. After each expanded candidate it divides the count after the previous expansion
by the current count (double precision; 0/0 is NaN and not saturated); a ratio >= patience_s is a saturated
expansion. When more than patience_p expansions in a row are saturated, the search stops before the next pop.
The defaults the paper uses are those of Lucene's PatienceKnnVectorQuery: s = 0.995, p = max(7, floor(0.3k)).
patience_queue chooses what "the queue" is, since here the beam width ef and the k_i results a shard returns
differ:
  0 = ef: an auxiliary max-heap of the efSearch smallest distances computed so far (k = ef);
  1 = ki: the result heap (k = k_i).
patience_p = 0 (default) disables it: no extra heap is kept and the search is unchanged.

Patience also decides only when to stop, so a run with (s, p, queue) equals the gamma = 0 search cut at the
first expansion where it fires. For offline replay, trace_ins_ef / trace_ins_ki add two columns to the 0002
trace: per row, the cumulative insertions into each queue at that point (after the previous expansion).

